---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro';
import { doc, COUNTS } from '../../data/site';
// src/settings/TranslationSettings.tsx, src/overlay/OverlayView.tsx, src/hooks/useTranslation.ts,
// src-tauri/src/translation/opus_mt_registry.rs (22 models, English to and from 11 languages, about 310 MB each).
---

<Guide
  slug="live-call-translation"
  lede="NexQ writes down what each side says and shows a translation under each line. It can do that offline between English and 11 other languages, or with an online service for many more."
>
  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <p>
    The call is transcribed first, and each finished line is translated. So you read the translation a moment after the
    sentence ends, like subtitles. NexQ doesn’t speak it, and the other side doesn’t see it.
  </p>
  <p>Both sides of the call are translated into the one language you choose.</p>

  <h2>Choose what translates</h2>
  <div class="guide-table" role="region" aria-label="Translation options" tabindex="0">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Option</th><th scope="col">Where your text goes</th><th scope="col">What it needs</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">OPUS-MT</th><td>Stays on your computer</td><td>One model per direction, about 310 MB each</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">LLM Translation</th>
          <td>To the AI you chose. With Ollama or LM Studio it stays on your computer</td>
          <td>An AI provider set up in NexQ</td>
        </tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">DeepL</th><td>To DeepL</td><td>Your DeepL key</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Google Translate</th><td>To Google</td><td>Your Google Cloud key</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Microsoft Translator</th><td>To Microsoft</td><td>Your Azure key and region</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Those are all {COUNTS.translation}. OPUS-MT has models from English and to English for Spanish, French, German, Italian,
    Portuguese, Dutch, Russian, Chinese, Arabic, Japanese and Korean. For Spanish to German, or a language that isn’t
    listed, use one of the others.
  </p>

  <h2>Set it up offline</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Choose a speech engine that covers the language being spoken. For a fully local setup, start with the
      <a class="link" href="../local-call-transcription-windows/">Windows</a> or
      <a class="link" href="../transcribe-calls-on-a-mac/">Mac</a> guide.
    </li>
    <li>
      If the two sides speak different languages, set <strong>Language</strong> on each card in
      <strong>Settings, Audio & Devices</strong>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Open <strong>Settings, Translation</strong>, choose <strong>OPUS-MT</strong>, download the direction you need, for
      example German to English, and set it as the active model.
    </li>
    <li>
      Press <strong>Set OPUS-MT as Active Translation Provider</strong> and set <strong>Target Language</strong> to match.
    </li>
    <li>
      Start a meeting and press <strong>Translate</strong> at the top of the overlay. <strong>Inline</strong> puts the
      translation under each line. <strong>Hover</strong> shows it when you point at a line.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Turned it on late? <strong>Translate All</strong> does the earlier lines. You can also select a few words and translate
    only those.
  </p>

  <h2>Honest limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      A wrong word in the transcript becomes a wrong translation. A good speech engine for the language matters more than
      the translator.
    </li>
    <li>
      OPUS-MT works in one direction at a time. With German to English active, lines that are already English go through
      the same model and can come out odd. If both languages are spoken, use one of the other options.
    </li>
    <li>
      Parakeet doesn’t cover Chinese, Japanese, Korean or Arabic. The
      <a class="link" href={doc('ai-providers')}>providers guide</a> lists what does.
    </li>
    <li>Use it to follow a conversation. For anything legal or medical, get an interpreter.</li>
  </ul>
</Guide>
